WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.868 --> 00:00:03.100
-Ladies and gentlemen,
please welcome Kieran Culkin!

00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:04.267
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:04.267 --> 00:00:06.033
-♪ Hustlin' is
the key to success ♪

00:00:06.033 --> 00:00:07.901
♪ Money is the key to success ♪

00:00:07.901 --> 00:00:11.200
♪ The life is getting lit,
sipping more, getting blessed ♪

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:13.801
♪ The games people play,
the games people play ♪

00:00:13.801 --> 00:00:15.367
♪ Success is just an ordinary ♪

00:00:15.367 --> 00:00:17.267
♪ Money is the key to success ♪

00:00:17.267 --> 00:00:21.033
-Come on.

00:00:21.033 --> 00:00:22.767
Come on, bud. It's --

00:00:22.767 --> 00:00:24.234
-Crazy!
-It's crazy.

00:00:24.234 --> 00:00:26.300
It's so good to see you.
-Thank you.

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:27.901
-We love you. Obviously,
the audience loves you.

00:00:27.901 --> 00:00:29.067
You got a big standing O, man.

00:00:29.067 --> 00:00:30.701
I didn't even get
a standing O when I came in.

00:00:30.701 --> 00:00:32.567
-There was, like, a sign
that says "stand up," right?

00:00:32.567 --> 00:00:34.000
-No, we don't have
a "stand up" sign.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:35.734
-No? Okay.
Just an "applause" sign.

00:00:35.734 --> 00:00:37.934
-Trust me, for the monologue,
they didn't stand up for me.

00:00:37.934 --> 00:00:40.968
-I saw it. It was good.
-Yeah, right.

00:00:40.968 --> 00:00:42.601
You're hosting
"Saturday Night Live" tomorrow.

00:00:42.601 --> 00:00:44.033
-Yeah, it's crazy. Yeah.

00:00:44.033 --> 00:00:46.133
It's absolutely insane.

00:00:46.133 --> 00:00:48.601
-Yeah, so thank you for
coming down, taking the time.

00:00:48.601 --> 00:00:49.701
-Thank you for having me.

00:00:49.701 --> 00:00:52.067
It's like -- it's been
a dream of mine

00:00:52.067 --> 00:00:54.667
and it's just surreal,
and everyone says, like,

00:00:54.667 --> 00:00:57.367
"Just try to capture moments,"
or something,

00:00:57.367 --> 00:00:59.133
because it just moves so fast.

00:00:59.133 --> 00:01:01.434
-Yeah.
-And I can't really do that,

00:01:01.434 --> 00:01:02.801
'cause there's just so much
work to be done.

00:01:02.801 --> 00:01:05.434
Like, we did a table read
and there was, like 38 scripts.

00:01:05.434 --> 00:01:07.734
And I get about halfway through
and...

00:01:07.734 --> 00:01:09.868
-It's so much work,
but you know what's great,

00:01:09.868 --> 00:01:11.100
is at the end --

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:13.434
I always feel like
at the end of the whole show

00:01:13.434 --> 00:01:14.901
when the band's playing
the good nights,

00:01:14.901 --> 00:01:17.801
it's actually -- you're just
hugging, you're saying goodbye,

00:01:17.801 --> 00:01:19.567
that show's in the can.

00:01:19.567 --> 00:01:21.567
It's live.
So it's just awesome.

00:01:21.567 --> 00:01:23.767
It feels great, and you're
like, "Whew. All that work.

00:01:23.767 --> 00:01:26.100
-Must be a nice moment.

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:27.300
I mean,
you were part of the cast

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:28.734
and then you've also
done hosting duty.

00:01:28.734 --> 00:01:30.734
Is there, like, a huge
difference between the two?

00:01:30.734 --> 00:01:32.834
For you?
-No.

00:01:32.834 --> 00:01:34.667
-Really?
-I don't remember, you know?

00:01:34.667 --> 00:01:36.133
I just kind of
remember the ride,

00:01:36.133 --> 00:01:39.234
and I was so green and learning
when I was just on the cast,

00:01:39.234 --> 00:01:41.601
but then hosting, you're kind of
going back to school

00:01:41.601 --> 00:01:44.901
and you remember the smell of
the paint from the studio.

00:01:44.901 --> 00:01:46.367
-Yeah.
-It's interesting.

00:01:46.367 --> 00:01:48.067
-You're a bit at an advantage,
I would assume,

00:01:48.067 --> 00:01:49.734
because as the host,
I'm given, like,

00:01:49.734 --> 00:01:52.033
so much stuff to do, but I also
have never done it before,

00:01:52.033 --> 00:01:53.601
and I'm just told to, like,
keep pace with everyone

00:01:53.601 --> 00:01:55.634
who's been doing it so well
for so long.

00:01:55.634 --> 00:01:57.000
-Just trust in Donna.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:58.901
-I've heard.
I've heard about Donna.

00:01:58.901 --> 00:02:01.167
-She'll take care of you.
She will take care of you.

00:02:01.167 --> 00:02:02.767
-But this isn't the first time

00:02:02.767 --> 00:02:04.167
you were on "Saturday
Night Live," my friend.

00:02:04.167 --> 00:02:06.000
-No.
-I did some research here.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:07.133
Some crack research.

00:02:07.133 --> 00:02:09.501
Look at you, you were here
with Dana Carvey.

00:02:09.501 --> 00:02:11.534
-Yeah!
-This is 1991.

00:02:11.534 --> 00:02:12.701
-That's me.

00:02:12.701 --> 00:02:13.901
-What do you remember
about this?

00:02:13.901 --> 00:02:15.834
-I actually remember
quite a lot from that.

00:02:15.834 --> 00:02:18.100
By the way, it was 30 years ago,
almost to the day.

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:19.133
I had to look it up.

00:02:19.133 --> 00:02:21.400
My brother hosted
in November of '91.

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:23.734
-Wow.
-It's crazy.

00:02:23.734 --> 00:02:25.834
There's little snippets
I remember.

00:02:25.834 --> 00:02:28.100
I was trying to think about it,
like walking around the studio

00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:29.400
and someone saying, like,

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:30.634
"Does it look smaller
than you remember?"

00:02:30.634 --> 00:02:33.100
I'm like, "No, it's just --"
-"I was smaller."

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:35.534
-I was smaller,
so it already looked huge.

00:02:35.534 --> 00:02:39.534
But I remember, like,
during rehearsal day,

00:02:39.534 --> 00:02:41.400
being on the stage
and I was with my mom

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:44.934
and Kevin Nealon walks by,
and she grabs him and she goes,

00:02:44.934 --> 00:02:46.100
"Oh, hey, you're great.

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:48.067
By the way,
you're my son's favorite."

00:02:48.067 --> 00:02:50.701
And he goes,
"Oh, thank you very much.

00:02:50.701 --> 00:02:51.634
That's nice to hear."

00:02:51.634 --> 00:02:53.934
And I went, "Mom," 'cause I'm 9.

00:02:53.934 --> 00:02:54.968
Kids don't have a filter.

00:02:54.968 --> 00:02:56.934
"Mom, I told you,
he's my second favorite.

00:02:56.934 --> 00:02:58.534
Dana Carvey's my favorite."

00:02:58.534 --> 00:03:00.467
[ Sour note plays ]
-Oh, my God.

00:03:00.467 --> 00:03:01.801
You said that to Kevin?

00:03:01.801 --> 00:03:04.968
-I did, and I remember
actually seeing him

00:03:04.968 --> 00:03:07.434
like, take this little,
tiny dagger and go,

00:03:07.434 --> 00:03:10.067
"Well, still very nice
to meet you."

00:03:10.067 --> 00:03:12.467
-He's a funny dude, too.
-Yeah, he's good.

00:03:12.467 --> 00:03:14.267
He picked me up at
the end of the show.

00:03:14.267 --> 00:03:15.334
-Did he really?

00:03:15.334 --> 00:03:16.701
-Yeah, I think I asked
him to do it.

00:03:16.701 --> 00:03:18.033
I hope so.

00:03:18.033 --> 00:03:19.167
I hope I gave him permission.
-You did. Yeah, yeah.

00:03:19.167 --> 00:03:22.067
Last time you were here,
you and your wife

00:03:22.067 --> 00:03:23.934
were weeks away
from having your first child.

00:03:23.934 --> 00:03:24.901
-Yeah.

00:03:24.901 --> 00:03:25.834
-And you now you have two
children.

00:03:25.834 --> 00:03:26.868
Congratulations.
-Thank you.

00:03:26.868 --> 00:03:28.400
We have two kids, yeah.
It's nuts.

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:29.601
Life is nuts.

00:03:29.601 --> 00:03:32.033
-Life moves fast,
like you were saying.

00:03:32.033 --> 00:03:33.367
But do you see a difference,

00:03:33.367 --> 00:03:35.300
having two children
as opposed to one?

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:36.701
-Well, yeah. Yeah.

00:03:36.701 --> 00:03:38.100
So...

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:39.234
But it's not that bad.

00:03:39.234 --> 00:03:40.300
I've had friends say, like,
"By the way,

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:41.934
having two kids is not
twice as hard,

00:03:41.934 --> 00:03:44.667
is exponentially way harder."

00:03:44.667 --> 00:03:46.100
But I haven't found that
to be the case.

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:48.167
It's sort of like the first kid
changes your life entirely

00:03:48.167 --> 00:03:50.601
and the second kid is just
more of the same.

00:03:50.601 --> 00:03:52.567
You know, it's kind of that.
-You're already in the madness.

00:03:52.567 --> 00:03:53.801
-Yeah, we're sort of
already in it.

00:03:53.801 --> 00:03:55.968
I remember when I was
on your show last,

00:03:55.968 --> 00:03:57.801
we were talking about
my wife, who was pregnant,

00:03:57.801 --> 00:03:59.033
and it was a little bit odd,

00:03:59.033 --> 00:04:01.667
because my wife pretty openly
hates children.

00:04:01.667 --> 00:04:02.701
[ Laughter ]

00:04:02.701 --> 00:04:04.501
She doesn't like other kids,
babies...

00:04:04.501 --> 00:04:06.434
-That's a problem, yeah.
-Just doesn't like them.

00:04:06.434 --> 00:04:08.734
It was like,
"I hope I like my kid."

00:04:08.734 --> 00:04:10.234
I'm like,
"I'm sure you will, honey."

00:04:10.234 --> 00:04:11.267
And she did.

00:04:11.267 --> 00:04:13.234
By the way,
so now we're two kids in,

00:04:13.234 --> 00:04:15.868
she still doesn't like kids.

00:04:15.868 --> 00:04:17.634
-She loves your kids.
-She loves our kids.

00:04:17.634 --> 00:04:18.834
And she likes, you know,
good kids.

00:04:18.834 --> 00:04:19.901
There's a thing, man.

00:04:19.901 --> 00:04:21.467
There's good kids
and there's bad kids.

00:04:21.467 --> 00:04:23.000
Just, it exists.
-Oh, yeah.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:24.634
Oh, it's real.
-Like, right away.

00:04:24.634 --> 00:04:25.667
Like, at a year and a half,
you're like,

00:04:25.667 --> 00:04:27.000
"That kid sucks."

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:29.033
[ Laughter ]

00:04:29.033 --> 00:04:31.667
"That kid's gonna be
someone awful."

00:04:31.667 --> 00:04:33.067
-That is so funny.

00:04:33.067 --> 00:04:38.133
-And she has such a, like,
temper for terrible kids.

00:04:38.133 --> 00:04:40.133
There was this one kid who --

00:04:40.133 --> 00:04:43.300
My daughter was about one,
almost one.

00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:44.567
She was just learning to walk,

00:04:44.567 --> 00:04:46.367
and you know at that age,
you have kids --

00:04:46.367 --> 00:04:48.467
Like, when they're just
learning to walk,

00:04:48.467 --> 00:04:50.400
they kind of like those
things that you grab hold to

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:52.801
and that have wheels so it kind
of helps them learn to walk.

00:04:52.801 --> 00:04:54.968
So she was just kind of
unsteady on her feet,

00:04:54.968 --> 00:04:56.801
and she went to this other kid
at the playground --

00:04:56.801 --> 00:04:58.601
it was like a truck
or something.

00:04:58.601 --> 00:05:00.601
The kid was about
three and a half,

00:05:00.601 --> 00:05:02.634
something like that,
and she grabbed his truck.

00:05:02.634 --> 00:05:04.100
The kid came over and goes,
"No!"

00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:06.567
And took the hands off
and he pushed my daughter

00:05:06.567 --> 00:05:07.868
to the ground.

00:05:07.868 --> 00:05:09.167
My wife didn't see this.

00:05:09.167 --> 00:05:12.033
I saw it, so I got angry
and I ran over

00:05:12.033 --> 00:05:13.767
and she's crying
and I'm holding her.

00:05:13.767 --> 00:05:15.801
I'm trying to keep my composure,
because, you know,

00:05:15.801 --> 00:05:18.133
I'm trying to reason with
a three-year-old.

00:05:18.133 --> 00:05:20.400
So I'm trying to tell him,
"You can't do that,"

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:21.934
and he goes, "Well, I don't want
her touching my thing."

00:05:21.934 --> 00:05:23.267
I was like,
"Well, that doesn't matter.

00:05:23.267 --> 00:05:24.567
You don't push people."

00:05:24.567 --> 00:05:25.901
And he goes, "I didn't
want her touching --"

00:05:25.901 --> 00:05:27.801
And then I start going,
"Oh, yeah? Well, look at me.

00:05:27.801 --> 00:05:29.400
I'm touching your truck!

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:31.000
You want to push me?"
-Oh, no!

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:32.334
-"Push me!"
-Oh, my God!

00:05:32.334 --> 00:05:33.667
-Yeah!
-You lost your mind.

00:05:33.667 --> 00:05:35.767
-And then I see my wife
coming over and I'm thinking

00:05:35.767 --> 00:05:37.133
she's gonna be
the voice of reason, right?

00:05:37.133 --> 00:05:38.567
Like, I'm thinking she's
gonna calm me down.

00:05:38.567 --> 00:05:40.467
The moment I see her walking,
"She's gonna calm me down."

00:05:40.467 --> 00:05:41.634
Instead, what she does is

00:05:41.634 --> 00:05:43.501
she goes to this
poor, little kid

00:05:43.501 --> 00:05:44.534
she picks up his truck,

00:05:44.534 --> 00:05:45.767
she moves it like
five feet from him.

00:05:45.767 --> 00:05:48.667
She goes, "You know what?
Why don't you...

00:05:48.667 --> 00:05:50.467
[bleep]!"

00:05:50.467 --> 00:05:53.434
[ Laughter ]

00:05:53.434 --> 00:05:57.033
Sorry, I know I can't
say that on this show.

00:05:57.033 --> 00:05:59.334
-Totally fine.
-Yeah. Sorry.

00:05:59.334 --> 00:06:01.968
Long story short.
-Kids are fantastic.

00:06:01.968 --> 00:06:03.033
You love them.

00:06:03.033 --> 00:06:04.200
I want to talk about
"Succession."

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:05.767
-Yeah.
-It's a big deal.

00:06:05.767 --> 00:06:07.067
[ Applause ]
-It's a thing. Yeah.

00:06:07.067 --> 00:06:10.000
-People like it.
-Renewed for a fourth season.

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:11.200
Congratulations on that.

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:13.801
You play Roman.
-Yeah.

00:06:13.801 --> 00:06:16.033
-But I heard that you originally
read for a different part.

00:06:16.033 --> 00:06:18.300
-They wanted me to read
for Cousin Greg.

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:19.968
-Really?
-Yeah,

00:06:19.968 --> 00:06:21.367
which I just didn't
feel right for.

00:06:21.367 --> 00:06:22.601
-I don't think...

00:06:22.601 --> 00:06:25.434
They made the right call,
by having you do Roman.

00:06:25.434 --> 00:06:29.000
-Yeah, well, they asked me
to read for Greg.

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:30.801
I knew I was wrong,
and I kind of --

00:06:30.801 --> 00:06:32.133
Normally, in any other script,

00:06:32.133 --> 00:06:33.534
I would be like, "Okay,
I'm not right for the part.

00:06:33.534 --> 00:06:36.100
I'm gonna close it. I'm not
gonna continue reading on."

00:06:36.100 --> 00:06:37.934
But I just kind of
liked the script, so I read on.

00:06:37.934 --> 00:06:39.267
I was like,
"Oh, there's this guy,"

00:06:39.267 --> 00:06:41.767
who's first line is something
I can't say on your show.

00:06:41.767 --> 00:06:44.734
-Eh, you already did.
-I already did.

00:06:44.734 --> 00:06:47.467
So let's just go full...

00:06:47.467 --> 00:06:48.667
-You're good at that.
-Yeah, well.

00:06:48.667 --> 00:06:50.200
He says,
"Hey, hey, mother[bleep]."

00:06:50.200 --> 00:06:52.767
-There you go.
-"Well...

00:06:52.767 --> 00:06:54.567
I kind of like this guy.
I like the way he talks.

00:06:54.567 --> 00:06:56.367
I can do that."

00:06:56.367 --> 00:06:58.000
I'll doing it on
national television right now,

00:06:58.000 --> 00:06:59.534
getting bleeped.

00:06:59.534 --> 00:07:01.968
-You nail it.
You're so great in that show.

00:07:01.968 --> 00:07:03.934
-I asked. I was like,
"Can I audition for Roman?"

00:07:03.934 --> 00:07:05.534
And then the response back was,

00:07:05.534 --> 00:07:06.934
"We're not auditioning
for that part yet,"

00:07:06.934 --> 00:07:09.567
but I just sort of put myself on
tape anyway and sent it in.

00:07:09.567 --> 00:07:12.200
I was like, "Here's three
scenes, if you want to..."

00:07:12.200 --> 00:07:15.434
And Nick Braun is fantastic
as Cousin Greg.

00:07:15.434 --> 00:07:18.234
-Of course.
The whole cast is fantastic.

00:07:18.234 --> 00:07:19.734
The thing that's great,

00:07:19.734 --> 00:07:22.701
because there's
certain people you love,

00:07:22.701 --> 00:07:24.701
but they're all bad, kind of.

00:07:24.701 --> 00:07:26.968
-Yeah.
-In kind of a weird way.

00:07:26.968 --> 00:07:28.400
-Yeah, but that's okay.

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:30.701
I like watching TV shows where
the characters are terrible,

00:07:30.701 --> 00:07:33.033
and then there's part of me
that feels like

00:07:33.033 --> 00:07:34.067
maybe there's something there?

00:07:34.067 --> 00:07:35.434
Like, maybe they won't
be terrible?

00:07:35.434 --> 00:07:38.601
Like, I find myself rooting
for Tom Wambsgans.

00:07:38.601 --> 00:07:40.033
I find myself going, like,

00:07:40.033 --> 00:07:42.033
I really want him to
stand up for himself and be like

00:07:42.033 --> 00:07:43.667
a really good guy
and then in the next scene,

00:07:43.667 --> 00:07:45.801
he's using a human footstool.

00:07:45.801 --> 00:07:47.968
And you're like,
"Maybe he's just awful.

00:07:47.968 --> 00:07:49.467
Why am I rooting for this guy?"

00:07:49.467 --> 00:07:51.200
-I want to show a clip.

00:07:51.200 --> 00:07:52.868
Here's Kieran Culkin
in "Succession."

00:07:52.868 --> 00:07:54.234
Take a look.

00:07:54.234 --> 00:07:56.567
-Okay, well, we can, uh...
talk about it over lunch, maybe?

00:07:56.567 --> 00:07:58.567
-Uh, not today, Roman.
I have a date.

00:07:58.567 --> 00:08:01.334
-[Bleep] off.
With who? Montgomery Clift?

00:08:01.334 --> 00:08:03.234
The ghost of Christmas past?
-Laurie.

00:08:03.234 --> 00:08:04.534
-Who?
-Laurie.

00:08:04.534 --> 00:08:05.701
He's ex-DOJ.

00:08:05.701 --> 00:08:08.901
He worked the Arthur Anderson
prosecution.

00:08:08.901 --> 00:08:10.667
I want to see if
there's a back-channel there.

00:08:10.667 --> 00:08:11.868
-Laurie.

00:08:11.868 --> 00:08:14.300
And you said he wants
to back-channel you

00:08:14.300 --> 00:08:15.367
in the parking lot, or...

00:08:15.367 --> 00:08:17.300
-Yeah, so the thing is,
I'm dating.

00:08:17.300 --> 00:08:18.434
I am dating.
-Mm-hmm.

00:08:18.434 --> 00:08:20.133
-And that needs to be
understood.

00:08:20.133 --> 00:08:23.334
-Okay. I got it. Yeah. Yeah.
You're -- you're drawing a line.

00:08:23.334 --> 00:08:25.667
Or otherwise, you know,
Laurie will --

00:08:25.667 --> 00:08:27.701
[In British accent ]
knock my jolly old block off.

